  {
    "episode_id": 64569,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64569.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Well, in New Hampshire, a lot of the campaigning is what I call, see me, touch me, feel me campaign. It is face to face. John McCain, for example, had 300 in some odd town meetings where a couple of hundred people would come and he would talk about what he was interested in and walk around afterwards and try and shake his many hands and look as many people in the eye. When George Herbert Walker Bush lost in Iowa, and we had 10 days in New Hampshire to pull it all together for him, I made him go to three, four, five, breakfast every morning. The press thought it was crazy, but he would go to McDonald's, Denny's Dunkin' Donuts and walk around, shake hands with everybody and talk to everybody while they were having a coffee and a donut. It is that personal contact that has to be a significant part of your campaign. It's not everything, but without that, nothing else you do up there will work.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
