        "text": "The interviewee has firsthand experience and credible insight into the political dynamics of the 1980s and 1990s.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The current political climate is sufficiently distinct from past eras to warrant meaningful comparison.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The interviewee possesses relevant judgment and analytical capacity to evaluate long-term political trends.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Public discourse benefits from historical perspective provided by former high-level officials.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker values reflective analysis over immediate reactions or partisan commentary.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 64569,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64569.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, look, there's always an evolution in process. I have to put it in the context of how presidents communicate. Franklin Roosevelt discovered the radio and went to his fireside chats to go directly to the people. And then John Kennedy discovered network TV, used it brilliantly. Bill Clinton discovered cable TV, tucked his saxophone under his arm and went on all those shows and talked directly to his constituency. Barack Obama was the first to make use of social media. He did it in a broad sense. And now you have Donald Trump discovering that every morning there's 50 million people who wake up to see what the dear leader has tweeted. And so this process of communication has become much more targeted to people. And yet more remote because you're not looking them in the eye. And as a result, I think it's caused much more of a sharpness to occur in the discourse. Everybody can have their own Twitter account and argue with 20,000 people that they've never even seen and be as obnoxious to them as they want and never suffer the response of a tough stare back. And if you're not looking at somebody when you're having this kind of an argument, you tend to be much more obnoxious, much more unwilling to listen to the other side at all and much more willing to come to common discourse. I've said that the problem in the country is not trying to figure out how to come together. The problem in the country is to figure out how we can continue to stand apart, but in a civil manner with civil discord. Of course, we're never going to obliterate differences. We're going to be apart in issues and desires for policy. But what we have to do is not try to figure out how to erase the differences, but how to argue for compromise in a civil way.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Presidents throughout history have adapted new forms of mass communication to reach the public.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The absence of eye contact during online arguments leads to increased rudeness and reduced empathy.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Franklin Roosevelt pioneered direct communication through radio via fireside chats.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "John F. Kennedy effectively utilized network television for presidential communication.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Bill Clinton leveraged cable television by appearing on talk shows with personal flair.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Barack Obama was the first U.S. president to broadly utilize social media platforms.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Digital communication reduces face-to-face interaction and increases emotional distance.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Donald Trump uses Twitter to reach approximately 50 million daily viewers every morning.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Modern communication methods allow for more targeted outreach to specific audiences.",
