  },
  {
    "episode_id": 64569,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64569.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, actually, more interesting piece of information. I'm the first governor in New Hampshire whose last name ended in a vowel, except for a couple of silent ease that were in my predecessors. When I went to Washington to talk to the consultants about running, they said, there is no way you're going to get elected in New Hampshire with a name like Sununu. Then 20 years later, my son got elected senator, and everyone said, well, the only reason he got elected in New Hampshire is his name was Sonna. So this name's work in funny ways. Look, immigration is an important component to the growth of America, but it's got to be legal immigration. My grandparents came through Ellis Island. They came through the process. I have lots of relatives that have come through the gateway in New York. And that's an important part of the process. This idea that we allow compassion to justify doing things in a way that are improper, I think is a false sentiment that is hurting the country across the board. We can have a compromise on immigration legislation if people stop trying to justify illegalities. And we have always been a country that has wanted to have a process that brings you through the door, not over the fence.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
